So the pid-dead fast path doesn't exist — dead pid still waits for the grace. The test expects immediate removal on dead pid. 

Which behavior do I want? The RCA says "a known pid the probe reports dead (fast path, no grace needed)". The grace exists to protect a process that may RESUME. A probe-dead pid cannot resume (pid gone). So immediate deletion is correct and matches the ticket's "checking pid via kill -0 where possible". 

But the pid-reuse caveat: probe says dead because the pid was recycled to a process we can't signal? No — kill -0 succeeding means SOME process (maybe recycled) has that pid → "alive" → keep. kill -0 failing means NO such process → the registering process is gone. Immediate deletion is safe.

